<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!DOCTYPE msxconfig SYSTEM 'msxconfig2.dtd'>
<msxconfig>

  <info>
    <manufacturer>Sanyo</manufacturer>
    <code>MPC-2300</code>
    <release_year>1987</release_year>
    <description>Soviet MSX2 for schools, with a Cyrillic keyboard, 128kB RAM and a network interface. The network interface is not fully emulated: hold DEL while booting to skip it and get to BASIC.</description>
    <type>MSX2</type>
    <region>ru</region>
  </info>

  <!-- WARNING: this config is partly guesswork!

       No real machine was available to us. The config is based on what
       we got from the following machines, assuming that they are all the
       same regarding hardware and configuration:

       - Serial 57501047 (of Alex Krasivsky, thanks a lot!):
         - the ROM dumps: BIOS/BASIC, sub ROM and network ROM
         - a photo of MSXMEM running on it (sent by e-mail in 2014): BIOS/
           BASIC in slot 0, sub ROM in slot 3-0, 128kB memory mapper in
           slot 3-2 and the network ROM in page 1 of slot 3-3.
         - photos of the bottom plate: "MODEL MPC-2300 (C8)", 220V, 50Hz,
           22W; and an inventory sticker "NO. 33"
         - photos of the top: the same keyboard (key caps, LEDs) as on the
           photos of the museum's machine (see below)

       - Serial 57501060: photos on https://www.msx.org/wiki/Sanyo_MPC-2300
         - Yamaha S1985 MSX-engine and Sanyo CF77099AFT on the underside
           of the PCB, V9938 VDP and NEC D780C-1 (Z80) CPU; no separate
           8251 or 8253 (for the network interface) seem to be visible.
           We found no information about the CF77099AFT, a custom chip;
           maybe it contains the network interface.
         - right side: 2 joystick ports and a cassette port

       - Serial unknown: photos of the Soviet Digital Electronics Museum
         http://www.leningrad.su/museum/show_calc.php?n=396&lang=0
         - Cyrillic (JCUKEN) keyboard with a RUS key (CODE, a lock key with
           a RUSS LED) and a CAPS LED, cursor keys, no numeric keypad
         - 2 cartridge slots; at the back: printer port, RGB, audio and
           video out, a small switch and a "SER PORT" connector (coax,
           probably for the network)
         - boot screen: 128kB VRAM and 128kB RAM

       What we derived from the ROMs:
       - The network ROM uses an 8251 at I/O ports 0x80 and 0x81, like the
         standard MSX RS-232 interface, so it is emulated with that. But
         it never programs an 8253 (it doesn't write to port 0x87): it
         only writes to ports 0x82-0x84 (strobes?) and reads port 0x86
         (the lower 6 bits, probably the station code). So the real
         hardware is probably different, e.g. with a fixed transmit clock.
       - The keyboard layout (ru unicodemap) was checked against the key
         tables in the BIOS (and against the key caps on the photos): it
         fits exactly.

       From the msx.org wiki (sources unknown): the release year (1987),
       and that it was used in schools (which fits with the network ROM).
       The wiki also says it is based on an MSX2 prototype of the Sanyo
       MPC-2 (Wavy2).

       NOT verified, i.e. guessed:
       - Taken from the config of the Sanyo PHC-23J (another Sanyo MSX2,
         with an S3527 MSX-engine according to its config):
         key_ghosting_sgc_protected, ignorePortDirections and the sound
         volumes.
       - Common for machines with an S1985 in openMSX: the I/O mirroring,
         the RTC and the printer port settings.
       - The network interface (see above), and the RAM of the RS-232
         interface; the purpose of the switch at the back.

       In openMSX, the screen stays blue at startup: the network ROM (with
       texts like "** TERMINAL MODE **", "INIT. WAIT ON" and "This STATION
       CODE =") initializes the 8251 and sends 4 bytes, but in openMSX the
       8251 only gets a transmit clock from a programmed 8253, so the bytes
       aren't sent and the ROM waits forever. (A photo of the museum's
       machine does show the BASIC prompt.) Holding DEL while booting
       skips the network ROM and gives BASIC.
  -->

  <CassettePort/>

  <devices>

    <S1985 id="S1985"> <!-- includes 5 bits mapper-read-back -->
      <sramname>mpc-2300_s1985.sram</sramname>
    </S1985>

    <PPI id="ppi">
      <io base="0xA8" num="8"/> <!-- S-1985 mirrors -->
      <sound>
        <volume>16000</volume>
      </sound>
      <keyboard_type>ru</keyboard_type>
      <has_keypad>false</has_keypad>
      <key_ghosting_sgc_protected>false</key_ghosting_sgc_protected>
      <code_kana_locks>true</code_kana_locks>
      <graph_locks>false</graph_locks>
    </PPI>

    <VDP id="VDP">
      <version>V9938</version>
      <vram>128</vram>
      <io base="0x98" num="8" type="O"/> <!-- S-1985 mirrors -->
      <io base="0x98" num="8" type="I"/> <!-- S-1985 mirrors -->
    </VDP>

    <PSG id="PSG">
      <type>YM2149</type> <!-- in S-1985 MSX-ENGINE -->
      <sound>
        <volume>21000</volume>
      </sound>
      <io base="0xA0" num="8" type="IO"/> <!-- S-1985 mirrors -->
      <ignorePortDirections>true</ignorePortDirections> <!-- not sure, but guess based on use of Engine IC -->
    </PSG>

    <RTC id="Real time clock">
      <sramname>mpc-2300.cmos</sramname>
      <io base="0xB4" num="4" type="IO"/> <!-- S-1985 mirrors -->
    </RTC>

    <PrinterPort id="Printer Port">
      <io base="0x90" num="8" type="IO"/> <!-- S-1985 mirrors -->
      <bidirectional>true</bidirectional> <!-- S-1985 feature (but not used, nor implemented) -->
      <unused_bits>0x00</unused_bits>
    </PrinterPort>


    <primary slot="0">
      <ROM id="MSX BIOS with BASIC ROM">
        <rom>
          <filename>mpc-2300_basic-bios2.rom</filename>
          <sha1>7850264dead5b05dd07fc79c1b7357940b92565e</sha1>
        </rom>
        <mem base="0x0000" size="0x8000"/>
      </ROM>
    </primary>

    <primary external="true" slot="1"/>

    <primary external="true" slot="2"/>

    <primary slot="3">

      <secondary slot="0">
        <ROM id="MSX Sub ROM">
          <rom>
            <filename>mpc-2300_msx2sub.rom</filename>
            <sha1>99990f4b262c6c6e0682041bf30d1ed24dc7dda5</sha1>
          </rom>
          <mem base="0x0000" size="0x4000"/>
        </ROM>
      </secondary>

      <secondary slot="2">
        <MemoryMapper id="Main RAM">
          <mem base="0x0000" size="0x10000"/>
          <size>128</size>
        </MemoryMapper>
      </secondary>

      <secondary slot="3">
        <MSX-RS232 id="RS-232">
          <io base="0x80" num="8"/>
          <rom>
            <filename>mpc-2300_network.rom</filename>
            <sha1>c7c27e2d5da18438f956edc2ca41536ad09d0e75</sha1>
          </rom>
          <mem base="0x4000" size="0x4000"/>
          <ram>true</ram>
        </MSX-RS232>
      </secondary>

    </primary>

  </devices>

</msxconfig>
